Central Credential Provider (CCP)
Windows Server installation, certificate authentication & prerequisite validation
Deployment engineering runbook | 23 October 2026 | Prepared for CyberArk Privilege Cloud / IDIRA
Scope: new on-premises Windows IIS CCP installation with TLS and client certificate authentication. This guide is not a substitute for the version-matched CyberArk release notes or tenant-specific Privilege Cloud connector configuration.
1. Executive implementation decisions
•	Preferred design: dedicated Windows Server 2022/2025 Desktop Experience with IIS 10 and a dedicated internal DNS name, such as ccp.company.example. Use only a CyberArk-supported installation package for the chosen OS and tenant.
•	Use a trusted enterprise CA-issued HTTPS server certificate and separate CA-issued client certificates for each consumer application or workload; protect private keys and plan rotation.
•	Separate two application IDs: internal AIMWebService AppID used by the CCP web service to communicate with its local provider; external consumer AppID passed in REST requests. Each identity has different authentication and Safe permission requirements.
•	Choose one client-auth pattern and document it: direct IIS client certificates (preferred when possible); alternatively a validated load-balancer implementation compliant with CyberArk forwarded-certificate settings.
•	Use certificate serial-number authentication for a straightforward initial rollout. Certificate-attribute authentication is also supported but its configuration is API-based.
