5.	Request a DNS A record or approved VIP name for CCP.
6.	Request a TLS Server Authentication certificate with exact CCP FQDN in DNS SAN and an export/provisioning method approved by PKI.
7.	Request unique Client Authentication certificates for each application/workload. Ensure client key is installed and readable only by the calling identity.
8.	Distribute enterprise root/intermediate CA trust chains. Validate CRL/OCSP reachability from CCP and callers.
9.	Restrict inbound 443 to approved applications/subnets/load balancer. Disable legacy SSL/TLS protocols according to approved baseline and validate cipher negotiation.
10.	If using a load balancer, document TLS passthrough versus TLS termination; do not trust arbitrary X-Client-Certificate or X-Forwarded-For headers from clients.
5. Install Windows Credential Provider (first)
11.	Obtain approved, matching Windows Credential Provider and Central Credential Provider Web Service media from the entitled CyberArk download location; check signatures, checksums and supported versions.
12.	Sign in using approved recorded administrative access. Stage the media in secured local folders (e.g. C:\Install\Central Credential Provider\Windows and ...\Central Credential Provider Web Service).
13.	Run the Credential Provider for Windows setup from the package first, following the package wizard and the Privilege Cloud integration instructions appropriate for your tenant.
14.	Supply tenant-specific registration, provider/installation identity and connection information required by that approved installation workflow. Do not invent or hardcode tenant credentials in scripts.
15.	Check the provider registration/connection status and installation logs. Verify the internal provider can communicate using tenant-supported architecture before proceeding.
IMPORTANT: The Credential Provider connects to CyberArk, while the AIMWebService is hosted in IIS. The web-service installation is not a replacement for provider installation.
6. Install the CCP AIMWebService (second)
16.	Use the Central Credential Provider Web Service folder from the SAME versioned package as the Credential Provider.
17.	Launch setup.exe elevated and complete the AIMWebService setup wizard. Alternatively run the package MSI in an approved deployment pipeline.
18.	Verify AIMWebService is visible under IIS > Sites > Default Web Site (unless customized).
19.	Confirm the application pool uses .NET CLR v4.0, which is the IIS runtime label for .NET Framework 4.x; ensure the appropriate app-pool identity is used.
20.	Inspect C:\inetpub\wwwroot\AIMWebService\web.config. Preserve default configuration and only change documented settings. Verify AppID matches the internal web-service identity.
msiexec.exe /i "<staged path>\AIMWebService.msi" /qn /L*v "C:\Temp\AIMWebService-install.log"
After installation, the CyberArk-documented help page is https://<CCP-FQDN>/AIMWebService/Help. The installation itself does not securely configure IIS; perform the next phase before production use.
7. Configure server-side HTTPS
21.	In certlm.msc, import the approved TLS server PFX with its private key into Local Computer > Personal > Certificates. Confirm chain, EKU Server Authentication and SAN name.
22.	Open inetmgr and navigate to Sites > Default Web Site > Bindings. Add/edit https on TCP 443 and select the installed certificate. If sharing port 443, validate hostname/SNI binding behavior before deployment.
23.	At IIS > Default Web Site > AIMWebService > SSL Settings, select Require SSL.
24.	On a dedicated certificate-only endpoint, set Client certificates: Require after client cert provisioning is ready. Use Accept only when intentionally allowing clients without mandatory IIS client certificate enforcement.
25.	Validate https://<CCP-FQDN>/AIMWebService/Help from an approved test client; certificate errors must be resolved, never bypassed.
26.	Enforce enterprise TLS baselines, certificate lifecycle, firewall scoping and HSTS as appropriate. Validate HSTS settings carefully on any shared site or subdomains.
8. Configure client certificates, mapping and client identity
8.1 Client certificate provisioning
27.	For each consuming application, have PKI issue a client certificate with Client Authentication EKU (1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.2), unique identity and valid date range.
28.	Install the client certificate and private key on the application machine or approved HSM/key store; never distribute PFX and password casually.
29.	Install appropriate client certificate issuing root/intermediate CA chain on the CCP machine; ensure IIS can build and revoke-check the chain.
30.	For each approved client, record serial number and certificate subject / SAN / issuer as required by the chosen CyberArk AppID method.
31.	Test client certificate presentation from the consumer to the IIS CCP endpoint.
8.2 IIS certificate authentication and optional mapping
32.	On IIS AIMWebService SSL Settings, set Client certificates = Require for a certificate-only endpoint.
33.	CyberArk additionally documents certificate mapping authentication role services and one-to-one certificate mappings. Install Web-Cert-Auth and Web-Client-Auth only when implementing that mapping approach.
34.	If implementing IIS certificate mapping, configure its appropriate user mapping, filesystem permissions and authentication settings with your IIS/security team, and disable Anonymous Authentication as required by the CyberArk mapping sequence.
35.	Do not turn on Windows Authentication solely to use certificate serial-number authentication. Windows Authentication is a separate optional endpoint pattern.
36.	If multiple consumers require incompatible IIS authentication settings, use CyberArk-documented separate web-service virtual applications / endpoints; retest all endpoints after upgrades.
Defense in depth: IIS TLS client certificate enforcement authenticates the TLS client at the web server; a consumer AppID certificate serial-number/attribute rule is configured separately in CyberArk. Do not confuse IIS authentication mappings with application Safe authorization.
8.3 OPTIONAL — Restrict client certificate Subject/CN through IIS Configuration Editor
Purpose: For environments that require additional IIS-side filtering before a caller reaches AIMWebService, IIS Client Certificate Mapping Authentication supports many-to-one mappings that compare Subject attributes (for example CN or OU) or Issuer attributes. This is optional and distinct from CyberArk AppID certificate serial number/attribute authentication. Implement only after an IIS/security design review: mapping associates a matched certificate with a Windows account and is not merely a stand-alone text filter.
Prerequisites and design checks
•	Confirm the AIMWebService IIS application/endpoint to secure (typically Default Web Site > AIMWebService); avoid applying settings globally to unrelated applications.
•	Confirm IIS Client Certificate Mapping Authentication role service is installed (Windows feature Web-CertProvider). Verify the feature name available on your Windows Server release before making changes. Active Directory Client Certificate Mapping Authentication is a different method.
•	Ensure valid HTTPS binding and trusted client CA chain; check certificate Client Authentication EKU, revocation reachability, and the required authentication scheme. Take a backup of IIS configuration and document rollback.
•	Create or identify a designated least-privilege Windows account for the mapping with only the necessary access. Coordinate password storage/rotation and IIS mapping credential protection with the Windows team; do not embed passwords in runbooks.
•	For a certificate-only endpoint, IIS SSL Settings must require SSL and client certificates. Review how IIS authentication (including Anonymous Authentication) interacts with chosen certificate mapping; test before production.
Configuration Editor — optional many-to-one CN match
37.	Open IIS Manager (inetmgr). Select the intended CCP site/application configuration scope and open Management > Configuration Editor.
38.	In Section, choose system.webServer/security/authentication/iisClientCertificateMappingAuthentication.
39.	Set enabled = True; set manyToOneCertificateMappingsEnabled = True. The one-to-one mapping setting is a separate option and is not needed for this CN-rule example.
40.	Open manyToOneMappings (ellipsis) and add a mapping: name = CCP-Approved-Ansible; enabled = True; permissionMode = Allow; userName = <approved mapped Windows account>; password = <supplied securely in IIS interface>.
41.	In the mapping item, open rules (ellipsis) > Add and set certificateField = Subject, certificateSubField = CN, matchCriteria = ccp-ansible-client.company.com, compareCaseSensitive = True (example values; use the exact PKI-issued Subject CN).
42.	Optionally add another rule for Issuer, such as certificateField = Issuer, certificateSubField = O and matchCriteria = <approved CA organization>. Confirm rule combination behavior in your test environment and never rely on a broadly shared CN as unique identity.
43.	Apply changes; confirm they are committed at the intended site/application scope in IIS applicationHost.config. Recheck Authentication and SSL Settings, then perform controlled recycle/restart only if required by your change procedure.
44.	Test three cases: approved client cert with matching CN (allowed if the Windows mapping and CyberArk AppID checks also pass); valid trusted cert with different CN (denied); and no client certificate (denied). Also test a matching CN from an unapproved issuer.
Example rule values (documentation placeholders, not a deployable credential):
•	certificateField: Subject
•	certificateSubField: CN
•	matchCriteria: ccp-ansible-client.company.com
•	compareCaseSensitive: True
•	permissionMode: Allow (in the many-to-one mapping, with the approved Windows account)
Important limitations and recommended alternatives
•	IIS CN filtering is a mapped Windows-authentication mechanism, NOT CyberArk application authorization. Continue configuring the consumer AppID with certificate serial number authentication or CyberArk-supported certificate attribute restrictions and least-privilege Safe access.
•	Matching a CN does not distinguish two otherwise trusted certificates using the same CN. For tighter identity control, use per-application certificates and CyberArk serial-number authentication; rotate/update the permitted serial when renewing certificates.
•	CyberArk certificate-attribute authentication is configured through its documented REST API and supports Subject, Issuer and supported SAN formats; do not invent a web.config setting to restrict certificate CN.
•	Do not use the ClientCertificateHTTPHeaderFieldName web.config parameter for direct client-to-IIS authentication. That setting is for carefully secured load-balancer certificate forwarding, where the load balancer controls header injection.
•	If mapping produces HTTP 401/403 or CyberArk reports APPAP330E, confirm IIS mapping identity, client certificate delivery and chain, then check AppID restrictions separately. Revert the optional mapping if it disrupts the already functioning certificate-auth flow.
