9. Configure CyberArk Privilege Cloud applications
9.1 Internal AIMWebService identity
45.	In Privilege Cloud/PVWA, go to Applications and add internal application AIMWebService (or your intentional custom web.config AppID).
46.	Configure permitted CCP host(s) under Allowed Machines using approved host/DNS/IP values.
47.	Configure the installed web service DLL path (default C:\inetpub\wwwroot\AIMWebService\bin\AIMWebService.dll) and/or internal IIS operating system identity as supported for your deployment.
48.	If using Hash authentication, calculate hash with the CyberArk NetAIMGetAppInfo utility according to CyberArk instructions and manage changes during upgrades.
49.	Validate the internal identity has only rights needed to service approved CCP requests.
9.2 External consumer application identity
50.	Create a distinct AppID for each caller, such as AAP-CCP-Prod. Do not reuse AIMWebService as the consumer application ID.
51.	Add certificate serial-number authentication (or certificate attribute authentication through the documented REST API). Use precisely formatted values.
52.	Optionally apply source Allowed Machines restrictions, recognizing load balancer forwarding implications.
53.	On the approved Safe, authorize the consumer AppID for retrieval/listing as needed, following least privilege; verify application access rights rather than granting Safe administrator.
54.	Identify the vaulted account by Safe/Object or approved search parameters; ensure the account object and platform are correct.
55.	Repeat per consumer; document owner, certificate expiry, approved Safe, app ID and renewal responsibility.
CyberArk serial-number application authentication accepts hexadecimal [a-fA-F0-9] and # characters. Certificate attribute authentication supports SAN/Subject/Issuer and has REST API-only configuration according to the reference guide.
10. Test and acceptance criteria
56.	Validate DNS resolves to expected server/VIP; network team verifies approved source-to-destination paths.
57.	From an authorized client with a valid certificate, load the Help page using HTTPS with trusted TLS.
58.	Invoke GetPassword from a test process with certificate selected and a least-privileged test AppID. Never print or save the returned password in logs or tickets.
59.	Test WITHOUT certificate: expect IIS access rejection when Require client certificates is enabled.
60.	Test WRONG / untrusted client certificate: request should fail. Test valid-but-unapproved certificate: CyberArk AppID authorization should fail.
61.	Test wrong AppID / unpermitted Safe/object: access must be denied.
62.	Review IIS access logs, AIMWebService/provider logs and Privilege Cloud audit events for correlated access without disclosing secrets.
63.	Restart IIS in a controlled test and verify recovery; rehearse certificate expiration and replacement, rollback and backup.
GET https://<CCP-FQDN>/AIMWebService/api/Accounts?AppID=<consumer-AppID>&Safe=<safe-name>&Object=<object-name>
The above is a request shape, not a ready-to-run security test. Use your application’s approved HTTP client with the client certificate and securely handle the response. An HTTP 200 alone does not prove intended access controls.
11. Troubleshooting matrix
Symptom	Probable investigation
TLS name mismatch	Inspect URL hostname and certificate DNS SAN / load balancer certificate.
403.7 / client certificate required	Verify client PFX/private key presented, IIS Require setting, client EKU and trust.
403 / certificate mapping failure	Review mapping roles, identity, configured mappings and IIS authentication.
Timeout to tenant	Check CCP/provider outbound channel, DNS, firewalls, proxy and documented endpoints.
Help URL not loading	Check W3SVC, IIS binding, app pool .NET v4.0, web app path, TLS and logs.
Application not authorized	Check two separate AppIDs, consumer certificate matching, Allowed Machines, Safe and object permissions.
Works direct, fails behind F5/LB	Validate client TLS termination, forwarded certificate header, trusted proxies and audit IP. Do not accept unsigned spoofable headers.
CRL/OCSP failure	Validate certificate chain and network access to certificate revocation services.
12. Operational handoff / change acceptance
•	Approved OS/patch level and vulnerability scan, endpoint/DNS, backup/DR and patching owner.
•	Prerequisite report CSV, exceptions, IIS role inventory, CCP/CP software versions and hashes.
•	Certificate inventory: serial numbers, thumbprints, certificate purpose, issuers, expiration and designated owners; exclude private keys from documentation.
•	Internal AppID and external AppIDs, Safe access approvals and periodic recertification.
•	Incident contacts, monitored logs, capacity baseline, certificate renewal process and rollback plan.
•	Positive retrieval, no-certificate denial, invalid-certificate denial, unapproved-AppID denial and negative-safe-permission tests.
